# EIP-7702 delegation routing patch (HOPEFULLY TEMPORARY)
#
# Ledger's DSK hardcodes `appName: "Ethereum"` for every device action, so DMK
# always opens the official Ethereum app before running the task. That app does
# not whitelist the Ambire EIP-7702 delegator (0x5A7FC11397E9a8AD41BF10bf13F22B0a63f96f6d),
# so it refuses to sign our delegation authorization.
#
# Until https://github.com/LedgerHQ/app-ethereum/pull/948 ("add EF-recognized delegators incl. Ambire")
# ships in an official Ethereum app release, we sign that one authorization with
# "Ambire Signer" - a fork of the Ethereum app that whitelists the delegator and
# is sideloaded onto the device (src/web/modules/hardware-wallet/controllers/
# LedgerController/ledgerSideload.ts).
#
# This patch therefore changes exactly ONE string, in ONE method:
# `signDelegationAuthorization` opens "Ambire Signer" instead of "Ethereum".
# Everything else (getAddress, signPersonalMessage, signTypedData,
# signTransaction - including the ongoing type-4 sends) keeps using the official
# Ethereum app. Patching rather than reimplementing the APDU keeps DSK's
# maintained 7702 TLV payload building and chunking.
#
# Both the cjs and esm builds are patched, since bundlers pick either one.
#
# REMOVE this patch (and the whole sideload flow) once https://github.com/LedgerHQ/app-ethereum/pull/948 is released.
